@page "/api-reference"

@* Route is /api-reference, not /api: ApiProxy claims /api/{**path} and forwards it to the
   backend, so a page mounted there would be unreachable. *@

<PageTitle>API | ksamods.gg</PageTitle>

<div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl">
    <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-primary">Reference</p>
    <h1 class="mt-3 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight">The ksamods.gg API</h1>

    <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl text-muted-foreground">
        Everything you can see on this site, you can read as JSON. Reads need no key and no
        account, because the whole point of an index is that other people's tools can use it.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-8 space-y-10 text-sm leading-relaxed">

        <section>
            <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-foreground">Basics</h2>
            <dl class="mt-3 divide-y divide-border rounded-lg border border-border">
                <div class="flex gap-4 px-4 py-2.5">
                    <dt class="w-32 shrink-0 text-muted-foreground">Base URL</dt>
                    <dd class="font-mono text-xs">/api/v1</dd>
                </div>
                <div class="flex gap-4 px-4 py-2.5">
                    <dt class="w-32 shrink-0 text-muted-foreground">Format</dt>
                    <dd>JSON. Every response carries <code class="font-mono text-xs">spec_version</code>.</dd>
                </div>
                <div class="flex gap-4 px-4 py-2.5">
                    <dt class="w-32 shrink-0 text-muted-foreground">Auth</dt>
                    <dd>None for reads. Writes use the session cookie from signing in.</dd>
                </div>
                <div class="flex gap-4 px-4 py-2.5">
                    <dt class="w-32 shrink-0 text-muted-foreground">Rate limit</dt>
                    <dd>300 reads and 60 writes per minute, per address.</dd>
                </div>
                <div class="flex gap-4 px-4 py-2.5">
                    <dt class="w-32 shrink-0 text-muted-foreground">Errors</dt>
                    <dd>
                        RFC 9457 problem details. Read
                        <code class="font-mono text-xs">detail</code> for something worth showing a person.
                    </dd>
                </div>
            </dl>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-foreground">Reading the catalogue</h2>
            <p class="mt-2 text-muted-foreground">
                No account needed. These are the endpoints a launcher or a mod manager will spend
                its time in.
            </p>

            @* min-w-[34rem] guarantees this overflows on a phone, so it guarantees a keyboard user
               needs a way in. *@
            <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto"
                 tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Endpoint reference, scrollable">
                <table class="w-full min-w-[34rem] border-collapse text-left">
                    <thead>
                        <tr class="border-b border-border text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-muted-foreground">
                            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Endpoint</th>
                            <th class="py-2 font-medium">What it gives you</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody class="divide-y divide-border">
                        <tr>
                            <td class="py-2.5 pr-4 align-top font-mono text-xs">GET /mods</td>
                            <td class="py-2.5 align-top text-muted-foreground">Search and list mods.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="py-2.5 pr-4 align-top font-mono text-xs">GET /content</td>
                            <td class="py-2.5 align-top text-muted-foreground">
                                The same search across mods and modlists together.
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="py-2.5 pr-4 align-top font-mono text-xs">GET /mods/{id}</td>
                            <td class="py-2.5 align-top text-muted-foreground">
                                One listing with its releases.
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="py-2.5 pr-4 align-top font-mono text-xs">GET /mods/{id}/releases/{version}</td>
                            <td class="py-2.5 align-top text-muted-foreground">
                                One release: download URL, hash, dependencies, validator findings.
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="py-2.5 pr-4 align-top font-mono text-xs">GET /collisions/{assetId}</td>
                            <td class="py-2.5 align-top text-muted-foreground">
                                Everyone declaring that asset id. More than one means they conflict.
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="py-2.5 pr-4 align-top font-mono text-xs">GET /builds</td>
                            <td class="py-2.5 align-top text-muted-foreground">Known game builds and their revisions.</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td class="py-2.5 pr-4 align-top font-mono text-xs">GET /modlists/{id}</td>
                            <td class="py-2.5 align-top text-muted-foreground">A modlist and its published versions.</td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>

            <h3 class="mt-6 text-sm font-semibold text-foreground">Search parameters</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-muted-foreground">
                All optional, all on <code class="font-mono text-xs">/mods</code> and
                <code class="font-mono text-xs">/content</code>.
            </p>
            <ul class="mt-2 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-muted-foreground">
                <li><code class="font-mono text-xs">q</code> matches name and short description.</li>
                <li><code class="font-mono text-xs">tag</code> filters to one tag.</li>
                <li><code class="font-mono text-xs">gameBuild</code> keeps only things with a release for that revision.</li>
                <li><code class="font-mono text-xs">limit</code> defaults to 50, capped at 200.</li>
                <li><code class="font-mono text-xs">after</code> is the cursor. See below.</li>
            </ul>

            <h3 class="mt-6 text-sm font-semibold text-foreground">Paging</h3>
            <p class="mt-2 text-muted-foreground">
                Responses carry a <code class="font-mono text-xs">next</code> value. Pass it back as
                <code class="font-mono text-xs">after</code> for the following page, and stop when it
                comes back null. This is a cursor rather than an offset on purpose: offsets skip and
                repeat rows when the catalogue changes mid-scroll, and anything taking a full
                snapshot would end up with a corrupt one.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-foreground">Resolving an install</h2>
            <p class="mt-2 text-muted-foreground">
                <code class="font-mono text-xs">POST /resolve</code> takes what you want and returns
                a concrete install plan, or an explanation of why there isn't one. It also reports
                asset id collisions between the mods you picked, which is the failure a player
                otherwise finds out about by noticing something silently missing in game.
            </p>

            <pre class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-border bg-muted/50 p-4 text-xs"
                 tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Example resolve request, scrollable"><code>POST /api/v1/resolve
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "content": [
    { "id": "OuterPlanets", "version": "1.2.0" },
    { "id": "AdvancedFlightComputer" }
  ],
  "gameBuild": 1042,
  "includeRecommended": false
}</code></pre>

            <p class="mt-3 text-muted-foreground">
                Leave <code class="font-mono text-xs">version</code> out to take the newest release
                that fits. <code class="font-mono text-xs">gameBuild</code> is the revision number
                from <code class="font-mono text-xs">/builds</code>.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-foreground">Writing</h2>
            <p class="mt-2 text-muted-foreground">
                Writes need a signed-in session and, for anything touching a mod, the right role on
                that mod. The cookie is HttpOnly, so these are for the site itself rather than for a
                script you're writing.
            </p>
            <ul class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-1 pl-5 text-muted-foreground">
                <li><code class="font-mono text-xs">POST /mods</code> creates a listing.</li>
                <li><code class="font-mono text-xs">POST /mods/{id}/repo-link</code> connects the repository releases come from.</li>
                <li><code class="font-mono text-xs">POST /mods/{id}/releases/import</code> pulls in tagged releases.</li>
                <li><code class="font-mono text-xs">POST /mods/{id}/releases/{version}/yank</code> retracts one build.</li>
                <li><code class="font-mono text-xs">POST /modlists</code> and the draft and publish routes under it.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-foreground">Two things to know before you build on this</h2>

            <p class="mt-2 text-muted-foreground">
                <span class="font-medium text-foreground">Download URLs aren't ours.</span>
                Every release points at a file on the author's own forge, and we store the
                <code class="font-mono text-xs">sha256</code> we measured when we checked it. Verify
                that hash after downloading. If it doesn't match, don't install the file, because
                the bytes changed after we looked at them.
            </p>

            <p class="mt-3 text-muted-foreground">
                <span class="font-medium text-foreground">Passing validation isn't a safety
                verdict.</span> Findings tell you an archive is packaged the way the game expects.
                Code mods are unsandboxed assemblies, and no amount of packaging analysis tells you
                what one does once it's running.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-foreground">The exported index</h2>
            <p class="mt-2 text-muted-foreground">
                If you want the whole catalogue rather than a query against it, there's a static
                export and a git mirror of it, both built to keep working if this one isn't.
                Polling <code class="font-mono text-xs">/mods</code> in a loop is the wrong tool for
                that job.
            </p>
        </section>
    </div>
</div>
